    <div class="resource-header">
      <span class="eyebrow">CBSE Class 10 · English Language &amp; Literature (Code 184) · First Flight</span>
      <h1>First Flight: Prose (All Chapters &amp; Board Exam Quiz)</h1>
      <p class="lead">
        Master all 9 prose chapters from the NCERT core reader with in-depth plot analysis, character motivations, central themes, stylistic devices, and 20 high-frequency CBSE Board Exam practice questions.
      </p>
    </div>

    <!-- Core Concepts Section -->
    <section class="section">
      <h2>First Flight Prose: Complete Chapter Breakdown</h2>

      <div class="grid two concept-grid">
        <article class="card">
          <h3>✉️ 1. A Letter to God (G.L. Fuentes)</h3>
          <p class="formula">Blind Faith in God vs. Ironical Suspicion of Humanity</p>
          <ul class="clean-list">
            <li><strong>The Protagonist:</strong> Lencho, an industrious farmer working like an ox in his cornfield, whose harvest is completely devastated by a sudden hailstorm resembling frozen pearls.</li>
            <li><strong>The Letter:</strong> With unwavering, child-like faith, he writes directly to God asking for 100 pesos to sow his field again and survive until the next crop.</li>
            <li><strong>The Postmaster's Altruism:</strong> The fat, amiable postmaster is moved by Lencho's faith. Not wanting to shake it, he collects contributions from his employees and friends, giving part of his own salary to raise 70 pesos.</li>
            <li><strong>Dramatic Irony:</strong> On counting 70 pesos instead of 100, Lencho does not doubt God ("God could not have made a mistake"). Instead, he writes a second letter demanding the remaining 30 pesos, urging God not to send it through the mail because "the post office employees are a bunch of crooks."</li>
          </ul>
        </article>

        <article class="card">
          <h3>🕊️ 2. Nelson Mandela: Long Walk to Freedom</h3>
          <p class="formula">Struggle Against Apartheid · True Meaning of Courage &amp; Freedom</p>
          <ul class="clean-list">
            <li><strong>Historic Inauguration (10th May 1994):</strong> Sworn in as South Africa's first democratic, non-racial Black President at the Union Buildings amphitheatre in Pretoria, attended by dignitaries from over 140 nations.</li>
            <li><strong>Definition of Courage:</strong> "Courage was not the absence of fear, but the triumph over it." The brave man is not he who does not feel afraid, but he who conquers that fear.</li>
            <li><strong>Twin Obligations:</strong> Every human has obligations to family, parents, wife, and children; and an obligation to his people, community, and country. Under apartheid, fulfilling both was impossible for a person of color.</li>
            <li><strong>Emancipation of the Oppressor:</strong> "The oppressed and the oppressor alike are robbed of their humanity." A man who takes away another man's freedom is a prisoner of hatred, prejudice, and narrow-mindedness.</li>
          </ul>
        </article>

        <article class="card">
          <h3>🦅 3. Two Stories about Flying</h3>
          <p class="formula">Overcoming Innate Fear (Seagull) &amp; Faith in Peril (Black Aeroplane)</p>
          <ul class="clean-list">
            <li><strong>Part I: His First Flight (Liam O'Flaherty):</strong>
              <br/>• <em>The Conflict:</em> A young seagull is terrified of the vast green sea beneath his ledge, believing his wings would never support him, while his younger siblings had already flown.
              <br/>• <em>The Turning Point:</em> Starving for 24 hours, his mother tears a piece of fish and halts just out of his reach. Driven desperate by hunger, he dives forward, falls into the void, and his wings instinctively spread open to conquer the air.
            </li>
            <li><strong>Part II: The Black Aeroplane (Frederick Forsyth):</strong>
              <br/>• <em>The Flight:</em> Flying his old Dakota DS 088 from Paris to England to enjoy breakfast with his family, the narrator encounters monstrous black storm clouds.
              <br/>• <em>The Enigma:</em> Inside the storm, compass and radio fail. A mysterious black aeroplane with no navigation lights appears; its pilot waves and guides him safely through the storm onto the runway.
              <br/>• <em>The Mystery:</em> The control room woman informs him that no other plane was flying that night, leaving the identity of his savior an unresolved supernatural enigma or a projection of his own subconscious survival instincts.
            </li>
          </ul>
        </article>

        <article class="card">
          <h3>📔 4. From the Diary of Anne Frank (Anne Frank)</h3>
          <p class="formula">Paper Has More Patience than People · Adolescent Wit &amp; Hiding</p>
          <ul class="clean-list">
            <li><strong>Motivation to Write:</strong> A 13-year-old Jewish girl living in Amsterdam who feels that despite having loving parents and 30 friends, she has no true confidant with whom to share her inner feelings. She names her diary 'Kitty'.</li>
            <li><strong>Family Background:</strong> Loving relationship with her grandmother ("No one knows how often I think of her and still love her") and migration history under Nazi anti-Jewish decrees (wearing yellow stars, restricted movement).</li>
            <li><strong>The Humor of Mr. Keesing:</strong> Her strict mathematics teacher punishes her talkative nature by assigning essays:
              <br/>1. <em>"A Chatterbox"</em> — Argued talking is a female student's trait inherited from her mother.
              <br/>2. <em>"An Incorrigible Chatterbox"</em> — Still couldn't stop.
              <br/>3. <em>"Quack, Quack, Quack, Said Mistress Chatterback"</em> — Wrote a satirical poem (with her friend Sanne) about a father swan who bit his three ducklings to death because they quacked too much. Mr. Keesing appreciated the wit, read it to the class, and allowed her to talk without further punishment.
            </li>
          </ul>
        </article>

        <article class="card">
          <h3>☕ 5. Glimpses of India (Three Regional Portraits)</h3>
          <p class="formula">Goan Heritage · Coorg Valour · Assam Tea Culture</p>
          <ul class="clean-list">
            <li><strong>Part I: A Baker from Goa (Lucio Rodrigues):</strong>
              <br/>• <em>The Pader:</em> Traditional village baker carrying forward Portuguese bread-making heritage. The jingling thud of his bamboo staff woke children who rushed for sweet bread bangles (*bolinhas*).
              <br/>• <em>Attire &amp; Prosperity:</em> Bakers wore *Kabai* (a peculiar single-piece long frock) and were prosperous, possessing a plump "jackfruit-like" physical appearance.
            </li>
            <li><strong>Part II: Coorg (Lokesh Abrol):</strong>
              <br/>• <em>Geography:</em> Smallest district of Karnataka, midway between Mysore and Mangalore, filled with evergreen rainforests, coffee estates, and spice gardens.
              <br/>• <em>Martial Race &amp; Kodavus:</em> Fiercely independent people of Greek or Arabic descent (wearing *Kuppia* resembling Arab *Kuffia*). The Coorg Regiment is one of the most decorated in the Indian Army, and General Cariappa was the first Chief of the Army Staff.
            </li>
            <li><strong>Part III: Tea from Assam (Arup Kumar Datta):</strong>
              <br/>• <em>Journey:</em> Classmates Rajvir and Pranjol travel to Dhekiabari tea estate managed by Pranjol's father.
              <br/>• <em>Legends of Tea:</em> Chinese Emperor discovering tea when wild leaves blew into boiling water; Indian monk Bodhidharma cutting off his eyelids to prevent sleep during meditation, from which ten tea plants grew. Peak harvest season: second-flush (May to July).
            </li>
          </ul>
        </article>

        <article class="card">
          <h3>🦦 6. Mijbil the Otter (Gavin Maxwell)</h3>
          <p class="formula">Man-Animal Bonding · Playful Eccentricity of Wildlife</p>
          <ul class="clean-list">
            <li><strong>Context:</strong> After losing his dog, Maxwell decides to keep an otter at Camusfearna in Scotland. He procures an otter from the Tigris marshes in Iraq (*Lutrogale perspicillata maxwelli*, later named Maxwell's otter).</li>
            <li><strong>Mij's Playfulness:</strong> Mijbil loved water—turning taps on with his paws, splashing wildly, and inventing games with ping-pong balls on the sloping lid of a damaged suitcase.</li>
            <li><strong>The Flight Ordeal:</strong> Packed into a 18-inch box, Mij tears the zinc lining and bleeds. On board the aircraft, he escapes into the passenger cabin, causing chaos before settling quietly on Maxwell's knees with the help of a kind air hostess.</li>
            <li><strong>Londoners' Bewilderment:</strong> Londoners had never seen an otter and made bizarre guesses: a baby seal, a squirrel, a walrus, a hippo, a beaver, a bear cub, and even "a brontosaur!"</li>
          </ul>
        </article>

        <article class="card">
          <h3>🚌 7. Madam Rides the Bus (Vallikkannan)</h3>
          <p class="formula">Child Curiosity · Independent Agency · Initiation into Mortality</p>
          <ul class="clean-list">
            <li><strong>Valli's Aspiration:</strong> 8-year-old Valliammai (Valli) spends hours watching the street from her front doorway. Her deepest desire is to ride the village bus that travels to the nearest town 6 miles away.</li>
            <li><strong>Meticulous Planning:</strong> She eavesdrops on passengers, learns the fare is 30 paise one-way (60 paise round trip), and the ride takes 45 minutes. She painstakingly resists temptations to spend on peppermints, toys, balloons, and the merry-go-round to save 60 paise.</li>
            <li><strong>The Journey:</strong> Takes the 1:00 PM bus while her mother naps. Conducts herself with dignified maturity ("I'm not a madam!"), laughs at a young cow galloping in front of the bus with its tail high in the air.</li>
            <li><strong>The Tragic Epiphany:</strong> On the return journey, she sees the same young cow lying dead by the roadside, struck by a speeding vehicle. The horrifying sight replaces her vivacious joy with somber maturity, initiating her into the sudden fragility of life and death.</li>
          </ul>
        </article>

        <article class="card">
          <h3>🪷 8. The Sermon at Benares (Betty Renshaw)</h3>
          <p class="formula">Universal Impermanence of Life · Liberation from Grief</p>
          <ul class="clean-list">
            <li><strong>Prince Siddhartha Gautama:</strong> Shielded from suffering until age 25, when seeing a sick man, an aged man, a funeral procession, and an ascetic monk prompts him to renounce his kingdom and seek enlightenment. He becomes the Buddha ("The Awakened One") and gives his first sermon at the holy city of Benares.</li>
            <li><strong>Kisa Gotami's Grief:</strong> A grief-stricken mother whose only son has died carries the corpse from house to house begging for medicine. Buddha asks her to bring a handful of mustard seeds from a house where no child, husband, parent, or friend has ever died.</li>
            <li><strong>The City Lights Realization:</strong> Finding no such house, Kisa Gotami sits at the edge of the city watching the lights flicker up and extinguish into darkness. She realizes human lives flicker and extinguish just like the lights; selfishness in grief blinds mortals to universal death.</li>
            <li><strong>Core Moral:</strong> "He who seeks peace should draw out the arrow of lamentation, complaint, and grief." Acceptance of mortal impermanence is the only pathway to peace of mind.</li>
          </ul>
        </article>

        <article class="card">
          <h3>💍 9. The Proposal (Anton Chekhov)</h3>
          <p class="formula">Russian Farce · Bourgeois Materialism · Petty Quarrels vs. Marriage</p>
          <ul class="clean-list">
            <li><strong>Characters:</strong> Stepan Stepanovitch Chubukov (landowner), his 25-year-old daughter Natalya Stepanovna, and their 35-year-old hypochondriac neighbor Ivan Vassilevitch Lomov.</li>
            <li><strong>The Proposal Premise:</strong> Lomov arrives in formal evening dress to propose to Natalya, motivated not by romantic love, but by practical convenience, age, social status, and his nervous palpitations.</li>
            <li><strong>Dispute 1: Oxen Meadows:</strong> Before proposing, conversation drifts to land ownership. Lomov claims Oxen Meadows belong to him; Natalya insists they are hers. Chubukov joins in and insults Lomov, triggering Lomov's palpitations until he storms out.</li>
            <li><strong>Dispute 2: Hunting Dogs:</strong> When Natalya learns Lomov came to propose, she becomes hysterical and demands he be fetched back. Upon his return, an even fiercer argument erupts over whose dog is superior—Lomov's 'Guess' or Chubukov's 'Squeezer'.</li>
            <li><strong>The Farce Conclusion:</strong> Lomov collapses unconscious from stress. Thinking he is dead, Chubukov panics. When Lomov revives, Chubukov hurriedly thrusts Natalya's hand into his, forcing them to kiss and be engaged, amidst which they immediately resume arguing about their dogs!</li>
          </ul>
        </article>

        <article class="card">
          <h3>👗 Reference: The Hundred Dresses I &amp; II (Eleanor Estes)</h3>
          <p class="formula">Anti-Bullying Theme · Injustice of Prejudiced Teasing · Redemption</p>
          <ul class="clean-list">
            <li><strong>Wanda Petronski:</strong> A poor Polish immigrant girl living at Boggins Heights, sitting in the corner of Room 13 in a faded blue dress.</li>
            <li><strong>The 100 Dresses Game:</strong> Peggy teasingly asks Wanda how many dresses she has. Wanda claims she has "a hundred dresses, all lined up in my closet"—prompting laughter and mockery from classmates.</li>
            <li><strong>Maddie's Moral Dilemma:</strong> Peggy's best friend Maddie is secretly poor herself, wearing hand-me-downs. She feels guilty for standing by silently during the teasing, fearing she might become the next target.</li>
            <li><strong>The Art Contest &amp; Letter:</strong> Wanda wins the school drawing contest with 100 exquisitely designed dress illustrations. However, her father moves the family to the big city to escape ethnic discrimination. Wanda writes a letter gifting her drawings to Peggy and Maddie, teaching them a poignant lifelong lesson in empathy and anti-bullying courage.</li>
          </ul>
        </article>
      </div>
    </section>

